/*!
\ingroup qmllint-warnings-and-errors

\title Signal handler name clash
\brief [signal-handler-name-clash] A property name is also a signal handler name.

\qmllintwarningcategory signal-handler-name-clash

\section1 Property is interpreted as a signal handler

\section2 What happened?
You declared a property whose name follows the \c{on<Signal>} pattern QML uses for signal
handlers, and the object also has a member the handler could belong to: either a signal
\c{<signal>} from the type itself or one of its base types, or a property \c{<signal>}
declared in the same object.

\section2 Why is this bad?
The signal handler interpretation wins. Any binding to such a property, including the
property's own initializer, is compiled as a signal handler rather than as a property
binding. A literal initializer makes the component fail to load with
\c{Cannot assign a value to a signal (expecting a script to be run)}, and an expression
initializer is silently run as a signal handler, leaving the property at its default value.

\section2 Example
\qml
import QtQuick

Item {
    property color primary: "green"
    property color onPrimary: "white" // not ok: read as a handler for "primary"

    signal accepted()
    property bool onAccepted: false   // not ok: read as a handler for the "accepted" signal
}
\endqml
To fix this warning, rename the properties so that they do not start with \c on followed by an
upper-case letter:
\qml
import QtQuick

Item {
    property color primary: "green"
    property color textOnPrimary: "white"

    signal accepted()
    property bool acceptedState: false
}
\endqml
*/
